<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bato the Daesitiate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 6–<a href="/years/9">9 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Bato of the Daesitiates</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bato the Daesitiate was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daesitiates">Daesitiates</a> of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and the principal commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-illyrian-revolt">Great Illyrian Revolt</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 6–9 CE. He fought alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bato-the-breucian">Bato the Breucian</a>, but had him executed in 8 CE for betraying the rising. After a long resistance he surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> in 9 CE and was sent into honourable exile at Ravenna.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bato-the-daesitiate.jpg" alt="Bato the Daesitiate" width="278" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LeonDardan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ilirski_bojevnik_-_Bato.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bato was probably born between 35 and 30 BCE in the lands around the upper Bosna river, the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daesitiates">Daesitiates</a>. From 33 BCE his people lived under Roman rule as a semi-independent peregrine civitas within the province of Illyricum, whose capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a> on the Adriatic coast. He probably came from a distinguished native family and, as an adult, probably held political and military office among the Daesitiates. He is said to have wished to emulate the Illyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardylis">Bardylis</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 6 CE Augustus planned a campaign against the Marcomanni in Magna Germania and ordered the mobilisation of Illyrian auxiliary forces. That spring the Illyrian forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> rebelled under Bato, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> the Breuci rose under another Bato. The two centres of resistance joined in the autumn of 6 CE, and the two leaders commanded an allied rebel army in the conflict the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-illyrian-revolt">Bellum Batonianum</a>, Bato's War.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> sent 10 legions and as many auxiliaries, allies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> under the future emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. Bato failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a> and, defeated by Marcus Valerius Messalla Messallinus, governor of Illyricum, withdrew north to join the leader of the Breuci. In the summer of 8 CE Bato of the Breuci surrendered to Tiberius on the river Bathinus, probably the Bosna; soon afterwards Bato of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daesitiates">Daesitiates</a> captured him, and his assembly had him put to death.</p><p>In 9 CE Tiberius and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> moved against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daesitiates">Daesitiates</a>. After hard fighting, Bato and his people surrendered in September, a few days before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</a>. Asked why they had rebelled, Bato is reported to have answered that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> were to blame, for they sent as guardians of their flocks not dogs or shepherds but wolves. He spent the rest of his life at Ravenna.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bato the Daesitiate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 8 CE Bato the Breucian surrendered Pinnes to Rome and was then executed by <b>Bato of the Daesitiates</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/breuci">Breuci</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Ravenna, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.42,12.2&amp;year=7" aria-label="Ravenna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Bato the Daesitiate"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5740 3741h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5740 3741h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=44.42,12.2&amp;year=7">What was here in 7 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
